A||N||13||DreXteR||Question for the engine Pros................................Not the usual question ;)||||1||
Z||000000||DreXteR||05-14-2001||10:36 AM||||Just wondering!!!!!!<P>Lets say, if we swap a <B>stock</B> 3.4 camaro engine into a Fiero. And we use the air plenum, Trottle body and stuff from the fiero. <B>Including the 2.8 injectors and fuel stuff from the Fiero.</B> This will end in less than the 160hp rated camaro engine.<P><BR><B>How will be the HP and Torque generated from this engine setup??</B><P>Just trying to understand where are the hp gained betwen both engines.<P><BR>DreX<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.centennialpr.net/drexter/Fiero/Fiero1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||05-14-2001||11:13 AM||||You are supose to use all that anyway. HP comes mainly from new engine higher displacement (3.4ltr vs 2.8lts). Don't know how bigger, if any, is the Camaro TB vs the Fiero. 3.4 injectors whith PROM for them should give stock (3.4) performance at least. I'm using 2.8 injectors with FPR and I haven't gone lean. I may switch to 3.4 injectors & PROM but I'm still not convinced on that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||05-14-2001||12:04 PM||||Unless you reprogram your ECM, a 3.4 will run lean with 2.8 injectors. Since the usual way of doing this swap is with the 2.8 intake system, it probably doesn't detract much from performance, if at all.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||05-14-2001||12:51 PM||||My 3.4 with 2.8 injectors, Hypertech (2.8) chip and FPR does not run lean. Maybe with a stock chip and it would have to be measured.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||05-14-2001||04:11 PM||||The intake and exhaust systems for the 3.4 Camaro and 2.8 fiero engines are almost identical. The 3.4-liter engine is 20% larger in displacement than the 2.8-liter engine. If you keep everything stock than you should have the 160 horsepower advertised by GM for the Camaro. My Dyno2000 program says a little over 160 horsepower. The biggest gain though is in the torque you gain over the 2.8-liter engine. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DreXteR||05-14-2001||06:19 PM||||Alex4mula, do you have any dyno sheet?? Did you notice a big diff between both engines?<P>I think that using the stock injectors from a 2.8 cant make the 160hp that supoust to make. Displacement doesnt do much w/o more fuel/air inside.<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||05-14-2001||06:30 PM||||If you run in moderate RPM & load ranges the computer will run the injectors w/ more duty cycle than a 2.8.  This is because it is a closed loop system using O2 sensor feedback and has 'room' to compensate.  If however you are @ WOT or very high RPMs with little load (let's say running 120 mph) you most likely will lean out your engine. Assuming 80% VE, and 6500 rpm a 3.4 needs 16.653 lbs/hr @ 100% duty cycle.||||reg||3
Z||000007||FieroHarry||05-14-2001||07:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>If you run in moderate RPM & load ranges the computer will run the injectors w/ more duty cycle than a 2.8.  This is because it is a closed loop system using O2 sensor feedback and has 'room' to compensate.  If however you are @ WOT or very high RPMs with little load (let's say running 120 mph) you most likely will lean out your engine. Assuming 80% VE, and 6500 rpm a 3.4 needs 16.653 lbs/hr @ 100% duty cycle.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree 100%, but what would the LBS/HR be for the 2.8 assuming the same volumetric eff.?<P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>FOCOSEVA,FOCOA<BR>1985 GT 262K+ <I>"TEMPTATION"</I>||||reg||1
Z||000008||ironhedno3||05-14-2001||08:00 PM||||im using all stock 2.8 stuff including injectors and chip and at 15,000 miles hadnt ran lean yet..if you try to use the camaro intake the throttle body is at the wrong end..19lb injectors and or more fuel presure will give you an increase but not mandatory||||reg||6
Z||000009||Alex4mula||05-14-2001||09:30 PM||||DreXter, I don't have dyno sheet. Can't compare with previous engine because it was really bad for a long time. What you will feel is the torque increase. I'm still doing a lot of tunning. Found out that my fuel preasure was sky high. Get the 3.4. You won't regret.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DreXteR||05-14-2001||10:28 PM||||Alex, I really havent decide what rebuild to do. For a 3.4 I will have to buy and rebuild it. I was thinking to make a 3.2 in a way its cheaper than the 3.4, cause is an upgrade from the existing one. <P>A 3.2 will use all the same that a 3.4 exept the piston diameter. Dont know how much it will influence in power, but its a cheaper way to get close.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanx for the imput.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||12:36 PM||||FH - Just saw your request.  I'll plug the #s in to my 'calculator' tonight and post them.<P>I just checked them and the answer is 14.101 lbs/hr.  I should note for those of you that are not aware, you can't run an injector @ 100% duty cycle for a couple of reasons.  The main one is that the computer would be railed in terms of injector control on the high end and most likely wouldn't be able to accurately control fuel up there.  This is why it is better to get an injector that flows slightly higher than what you need and then tune the computer for that set of injectors flow rate. In other words computer and injector must be in sync w/ regard to fuel flow assumptions.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||GT GT 87||05-15-2001||06:45 PM||||As long as the injectors flow what is asked of them you will never know the dif..  Add cam headers exhaust bored t.b. perhapes they won't flow enough? Just don't assume they aren't big enough. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||09:44 PM||||Actually, it is a calculation.  If you know the target A/F just above 14.7/1 and you know the eng flow, you know how much fuel is needed at a given rpm to make max pwr and not fry the engine.  Not only do you need to match the injector to the application but you need to match the computer's control of the injectors and the computer's assumptions of the engine's ability to pump air.||||reg||1
